  "stage_path": "/private/tmp/suiroai-dsc59/artifacts/dsc60-final-evaluation-2026-10-20/business-writing-v2/runs/business-response-b99ea044d5dc41e19b1c1af73ccd6b2f",
  "status": "rewritten",
  "text": "The delivered list recommends 880 units across 112 product-and-store combinations for the week of 1–7 December 2025. Start by reviewing the 52 flagged cases where stock could run short before a delivery arrives, then work through the proposed orders by store. The list gives you a starting point for purchasing, with quantities already worked out for each location.\n\nThe routine turns your review of recent sales into a repeatable calculation. It uses 26 weeks of recorded sales and blends each product’s local sales pattern with broader product and store patterns, helping steady estimates where individual items sell infrequently. It then estimates stock needed through the delivery wait and the next review period, allows for the supplied safety stock, accounts for stock on hand and incoming deliveries, and applies pack sizes and minimum order quantities. This automates the repeated arithmetic involved in turning sales history into an order list.\n\nIn the completed comparison for 1–28 December 2025, forecasts made on 30 November had 4.06% lower average error than a simple average of the preceding 13 weeks of recorded sales. Average error fell from about 1.08 to 1.03 units per product, per store, per week, with lower error in each of the four weeks. That is a modest measured improvement in forecasting recorded sales; it does not establish lower purchasing costs or fewer stockouts. The comparison also does not measure your exact buying process, since your usual adjustments and ordering rule were not specified.\n\nBefore buying, replace the illustrative stock, incoming-delivery and ordering-policy inputs with your real figures, and check delivery dates and pack rules. Check shortages even where the list recommends zero: an order placed now may arrive too late to prevent a gap. The forecasts tended to run below recorded sales overall, and missing sales or stockout records can hide demand, so give known shortages particular attention.\n\nTo use the delivered routine, run it locally with the supplied sales and stock, delivery and policy files, review the resulting quantities, and place the approved orders through your usual process. The existing dataset is enough to run the MVP. As an optional improvement, keep consistent records of stockouts, daily sales and actual delivery dates; these may help future estimates distinguish quiet trading from unavailable stock.\n\nThe measured local run took about 0.43 seconds and used about 140 MB of memory for this workload; timings will vary by computer. Routine runs need no paid AI or model service. Ordinary computer and electricity or hosting costs still apply. Those running costs are separate from the AI-assisted build cost, for which no charge information was supplied.\n",
